# Three registers, three very different rows — where does the data come from?
prompt: "What does one row really represent in npr, in lmr and in kuhr? Why were all three registers built for administration and reimbursement, not for research — and what does that do to your data? Why is 2007–2008 a watershed for the Norwegian Patient Registry, and what happened to the Prescription Database in 2022?"
next: {"title":"How do you read a 12 GB register file without crashing your machine?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/registerdata-i-praksis-python-og-pandas/hvordan-leser-du-en-12-gb-registerfil.cast"}
enroll: https://drawcast.anvil.app

## One patient, three rows
lang: en
voice: male
level: basic

If you count rows in npr and think you are counting patients, your prevalence is wrong before the analysis has even begun — this series is about where the rows actually come from.
    text title "One patient, three rows" x 500 y 700 font_size 36

We follow one person all the way: pid 100237, a pseudonymous ID number in our diabetes cohort.
    box pid "pid = 100237" x 500 y 622

    pause 1

In npr — NPR, the Norwegian Patient Registry — one row is one contact or one stay in specialist care: hospitals and outpatient clinics.
    box h_npr "npr" #b5482e x 175 y 535
    text npr_def "one row = one contact" #b5482e x 175 y 455 font_size 24

Her row says: admitted on the fourteenth of March 2019, discharged two days later, with the icd10 code E11.9 — type 2 diabetes without complications.
    text npr_v1 "dato_inn: 2019-03-14" x 175 y 395 font_size 23
    text npr_v2 "dato_ut: 2019-03-16" x 175 y 350 font_size 23
    text npr_v3 "icd10: E11.9" x 175 y 305 font_size 23

    pause 1

In lmr — Legemiddelregisteret, the Norwegian drug register — one row is one dispensing at a pharmacy: not a prescription, and certainly not a tablet swallowed.
    box h_lmr "lmr" #2f6b8f x 500 y 535
    text lmr_def "one row = one dispensing" #2f6b8f x 500 y 455 font_size 24

On the second of April she collected two packs of metformin, ATC A10BA02, together a hundred and sixty-eight defined daily doses.
    text lmr_v1 "dato_utlev: 2019-04-02" x 500 y 395 font_size 23
    text lmr_v2 "atc: A10BA02" x 500 y 350 font_size 23
    text lmr_v3 "ddd: 168 · antall_pakn: 2" x 500 y 305 font_size 23

    pause 1

In kuhr, one row is one reimbursement claim from a provider to Helfo, the Norwegian Health Economics Administration — in other words, a bill.
    box h_kuhr "kuhr" #8a5fa8 x 825 y 535
    text kuhr_def "one row = one claim" #8a5fa8 x 825 y 455 font_size 24

On the eighth of May her GP claimed fee code 2ad for a consultation, and in our extract no diagnosis comes with that claim.
    text kuhr_v1 "dato_krav: 2019-05-08" x 825 y 395 font_size 23
    text kuhr_v2 "takst: 2ad" x 825 y 350 font_size 23
    text kuhr_v3 "no diagnosis in extract" x 825 y 305 font_size 23

    pause 1

Three rows with different columns, different dates and different coding systems — the only thing that ties them together is pid.
    arrow a_npr pid -> h_npr #8f887c
    arrow a_lmr pid -> h_lmr #8f887c
    arrow a_kuhr pid -> h_kuhr #8f887c
    parallel true

Nor were they ever built to fit together: the Patient Registry was set up in 1997, the Prescription Database recorded its first dispensing on the first of January 2004, and KUHR was made to pay money out to providers.
    focus npr_def lmr_def kuhr_def

    pause 1

So let us count the rows this one person produced during 2019.
    clear keep ["title"]

Three rows in npr, eleven dispensings in lmr and twenty-four claims in kuhr.
    text c_npr "npr: 3 rows" #b5482e x 320 y 510 font_size 28
    text c_lmr "lmr: 11 rows" #2f6b8f x 320 y 450 font_size 28
    text c_kuhr "kuhr: 24 rows" #8a5fa8 x 320 y 390 font_size 28

    pause 1

Thirty-eight rows — and one patient.
    arrow a_sum #8f887c from.x 470 from.y 450 to.ref sum_node
    box sum_node "1 patient" x 740 y 450
    delivery grave

And notice which register contributes most: the sickest person in your table is often just the one with the most bills.
    highlight c_kuhr sum_node effect glow

    pause 1

That is why this is the first question you ask of every extract.
    text insight "What does one row count?" x 500 y 235 font_size 30
    mark mark target insight kind box

Three registers answer three different questions — and only when you know which question a row answers can you build the cohort.

## Built for payment, not for research
lang: en
voice: male
level: basic

    text title "Built for payment, not research" x 500 y 700 font_size 38 hidden true
    box n_syk "Hospital stay" x 140 y 545 hidden true
    box n_apo "Pharmacy pickup" x 140 y 400 hidden true
    box n_fast "GP visit" x 140 y 255 hidden true
    box pay1 "ISF payment" #b5482e x 480 y 545 hidden true
    box pay2 "Blue-script refund" #b5482e x 480 y 400 hidden true
    box pay3 "Fee claim to Helfo" #b5482e x 480 y 255 hidden true
    box reg1 "npr" #2f6b8f x 830 y 545 hidden true
    box reg2 "lmr" #2f6b8f x 830 y 400 hidden true
    box reg3 "kuhr" #2f6b8f x 830 y 255 hidden true
    arrow a1 n_syk -> pay1 #b5482e hidden true
    arrow a2 pay1 -> reg1 #2f6b8f hidden true
    arrow a3 n_apo -> pay2 #b5482e hidden true
    arrow a4 pay2 -> reg2 #2f6b8f hidden true
    arrow a5 n_fast -> pay3 #b5482e hidden true
    arrow a6 pay3 -> reg3 #2f6b8f hidden true

Now that we have seen what one row is in npr, lmr and kuhr, let us see why the row exists — because that decides which questions your cohort can bear.
    draw title

Kari, sixty-two, with type 2 diabetes, meets the health service in three places during 2023.
    draw n_syk n_apo n_fast parallel true

    pause 1

Two days in hospital in March, reported with E11 — and the report exists because the hospital's ISF payment is calculated from it. That is the row in npr.
    draw a1 pay1 a2 reg1

    pause 1

Four times she collects metformin, A10BA02, on a blue prescription; the pharmacy reports each dispensing and bills the state for its share, and the dispensing becomes the row in lmr, with dato_utlev and ddd.
    draw a3 pay2 a4 reg2

    pause 1

And at her GP, every visit turns into fee codes on the bill to Helfo — the bill is the row in kuhr.
    draw a5 pay3 a6 reg3

    pause 1

The middle column is the motive: none of the twelve rows about Kari were written to describe her; they were written to trigger a payment.
    focus pay1 pay2 pay3

That is more recent than many think — with activity-based financing, ISF, from 1997, hospital activity was turned into money, and coding became an economic act.
    label lbl_isf "ISF since 1997" above #8f887c attach_to pay1

    pause 1

    clear keep ["title"]

First consequence: Kari's four lmr rows say that the boxes left the pharmacy, not that the tablets were swallowed.
    text c1 "dispensed ≠ taken" #b5482e x 500 y 565 font_size 34
    text c1e "left the pharmacy" #8f887c x 500 y 518 font_size 22

    pause 1

Second: the fee code tells you what the GP billed for, not how ill Kari was that day.
    text c2 "fee code ≠ diagnosis" #b5482e x 500 y 410 font_size 34
    text c2e "what was billed" #8f887c x 500 y 363 font_size 22

    pause 1

Third, and the costliest: a Kari who manages on diet alone, or who gets her medicines in a nursing home, is not free of disease — she lacks a reimbursement claim, and drops out of your cohort.
    text c3 "no row ≠ no disease" #b5482e x 500 y 255 font_size 34
    text c3e "no claim, no trace" #8f887c x 500 y 208 font_size 22

    pause 1

All three are the same mistake: reading a flow of money as if it were a medical record.
    highlight c1 c2 c3 effect glow

    pause 1

    clear keep ["title"]

But it is precisely because the money had to be controlled that you get the whole population, with nobody having to remember anything afterwards, many years back.
    text h_str "Strengths" #2f6b8f x 270 y 555 font_size 32
    text s1 "Whole population" x 270 y 480 font_size 24
    text s2 "No recall bias" x 270 y 425 font_size 24
    text s3 "Many years back" x 270 y 370 font_size 24

The same logic gives you no HbA1c, no BMI, no adherence — and coding shaped by the reimbursement rules.
    text h_wk "Weaknesses" #b5482e x 730 y 555 font_size 32
    text w1 "No HbA1c or BMI" x 730 y 480 font_size 24
    text w2 "No adherence" x 730 y 425 font_size 24
    text w3 "Coding follows money" x 730 y 370 font_size 24

    pause 1

The registers know exactly who was paid for; you are asking who is ill — and the distance between those two questions is your whole analysis.
    text syn "Registers know who was paid for" x 500 y 230 font_size 28
    mark mark_syn target syn kind box
    delivery grave

## 2008 and 2022: the data has a history
lang: en
voice: male
level: basic
use: timeline
with:
    title: '2008 and 2022: the data has a history'
    start_label: The registers begin
    end_label: Today
    milestones:
      - {label: '1997', sublabel: NPR set up – de-identified}
      - {label: '2004', sublabel: Prescription DB – pseudonymous}
      - {label: '2006', sublabel: KUHR claims data}
      - {label: '2008', sublabel: NPR person-identifiable, emphasize: true}
      - {label: '2022', sublabel: Legemiddelregisteret (lmr), emphasize: true}

Now that we have seen that all three registers were built for payment and administration, one thing remains: the data also has a history, and that history can look like findings in your numbers.
    draw title line

The Norwegian Patient Registry was set up in 1997, but for its first ten years it was in practice a counter of hospital activity: events with no identity you could follow over time.
    draw dot_0 label_0

    pause 1

The Prescription Database, Reseptregisteret, arrived in 2004, and it was deliberately made pseudonymous — the privacy compromise that let the register be created at all.
    draw dot_1 label_1

    pause 1

KUHR, the claims from GPs and other providers, has full data from 2006, and is the youngest of the three sources we use.
    draw dot_2 label_2

    pause 1

And then, by a decision of parliament in 2007, the Patient Registry became person-identifiable from 2008.
    draw dot_3 label_3 delivery grave

That is the watershed: only then could two stays for the same person be linked over time — only then did npr in practice get a pid to link on.
    focus dot_3 label_3

    pause 1

If you compute the prevalence of E11 from 2005 onwards, you see a neat rise around 2008 — but that rise is the register, not the patients.
    highlight label_3 effect glow

    pause 1

In 2022 the same thing happened on the drug side: pharmacies began reporting to Legemiddelregisteret, which replaces the Prescription Database — the register we call lmr.
    draw dot_4 label_4

The pseudonym was swapped for a person-identifiable key, and the register is gradually meant to cover drugs given in institutions too — so the compromise of 2004 was reversed less than twenty years later.
    highlight label_4 effect glow

    pause 1

    clear

Let us take our cohort and set the study window to 2005 through 2010.
    text ex_head "Study window: 2005–2010" x 500 y 650 font_size 34

    pause 1

The dispensings are there: lmr reaches back to 2004, so a three-year washout before a first metformin in 2008 just fits.
    text ex_lmr "lmr: dispensings from 2004" #2f6b8f x 500 y 545 font_size 28

    pause 1

But E11 codes at person level only exist from 2008.
    text ex_npr "npr: patient level from 2008" #b5482e x 500 y 475 font_size 28

    pause 1

2005 to 2007 come out empty — not because nobody had diabetes, but because the register back then could not follow a person.
    text ex_gap "2005–2007: a gap in history" x 500 y 360 font_size 30

    pause 1

Hence the rule we take with us: choose your study window and your reading of it by what the register was at that time, not by what it is today.
    text rule "Pick the window by what the register was then." x 500 y 205 font_size 30
    mark rule_mark target rule kind box

    pause 1

One patient gave us three rows; the rows mean three different things; they were written for payment — and now you see that each of them also has a date when the register became a different register.
    delivery grave

## Where next
end_page: true

